WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.422 --> 00:00:01.630
SPEAKER: What's up, everyone?

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.005
Thanks for checking
out this week

00:00:03.005 --> 00:00:05.520
in Cloud, where we serve
you the lowest latency news.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.610
Recently, we announced Cloud
Services Platform in beta

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.160
to help you modernize your apps.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.910
This week, we're making
things even easier

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.190
with a beta for CSP
config management,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.290
allowing you to create a
consistent configuration

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.260
for your Kubernetes resources
in multi-cluster setups.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.430
It even uses the native
Kubernetes format,

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.480
such as XAML or JSON, making
it easy to get your existing

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.490
definitions applied to
all of your clusters,

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.073
as well as integrating with Git
to make continuous integration

00:00:29.073 --> 00:00:29.640
simple.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.680
Make a note to check out
CSP config management

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.300
as soon as you can.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.730
Between Google Search,
Google Maps, and YouTube,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.395
our products handle huge amounts
of data with high performance.

00:00:38.395 --> 00:00:40.020
The technology that
keeps these running

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.110
is available to everyone through
Cloud Bigtable, which now lets

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.000
you globally distribute data.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Cloud Bigtable is a managed
wide-column NoSQL database

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.160
that can scale from
gigabytes to petabytes

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.765
while still maintaining high
performance and low latency.

00:00:52.765 --> 00:00:54.390
With global replication,
you can create

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.960
multiple primary clusters.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.530
So if you're working with
IoT, finance, ad tech,

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.955
gaming, or just about
anything else, check it out.

00:01:01.955 --> 00:01:03.330
Speaking of huge
amounts of data,

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.890
Google Cloud Storage
is a great place

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.455
to store unstructured data.

00:01:06.455 --> 00:01:07.830
And you can choose
different ways

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.573
to store that data,
such as making

00:01:09.573 --> 00:01:11.490
sure it's served up
quickly using multi-region

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.190
or kept safe using
georedundant ColdLine.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.410
We're excited to share that
ColdLine is now cheaper.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.060
And we have a new
way to protect you

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.550
from unpredictable storage
growth, the storage growth

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.330
plan.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.080
You can commit to a
fixed amount per month

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.632
and not be charged for any
usage over your commitment.

00:01:25.632 --> 00:01:27.090
You can then renew
after 12 months,

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.760
and your overage is
free if it's within 30%.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.080
This is a great way to
better forecast your costs,

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.020
so try out Google
Cloud Storage and see

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.878
if you can save some cash.

00:01:35.878 --> 00:01:37.420
Now that you can
store all that data,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.090
the next step is
probably analyzing it,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.470
and that means BigQuery.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.360
We've just launched a
new data analysis quest

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.902
to help you get up to speed on
using BigQuery to troubleshoot

00:01:44.902 --> 00:01:47.370
common SQL issues and to
build your own custom data

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.660
visualizations.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.110
Even better, you
can use the code

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.700
3g-bigquery-785 to get
the first lab free.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.700
So get started on learning how
to query petabytes and even

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.380
start to build machine
learning models.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.027
Get questing.

00:01:59.027 --> 00:02:01.110
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.075
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:02:03.075 --> 00:02:04.200
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.740
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.150
We'll see you in the cloud.

